  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1024 AM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nDRY AND COOL FOR SUNDAY:\n\nDry and cool weather is expected for Sunday with a weak ridge moving \nthrough the Intermountain West. Mid to high level clouds will move \nin from the west through the day as our next storm system \napproaches. \n\nQUICK MOVING SYSTEM FOR MONDAY:\n\nMoisture will begin to move into the area Sunday night as a weak low-\namplitude trough moves through and brings southwesterly flow. \nThis system will be fast moving with moisture and lift remaining\nlimited. High-res models begin to develop light showers Monday \nmorning over the Four Corners. This will bring a rain/snow mix \nfor valley locations with mostly snow expected in the higher \nterrain above 6000 ft. There are some pockets of weak \ninstability that may develop Monday afternoon (SBCAPE 50-100 \nJ/kg) over southwest Colorado creating the possibility of \nlocally heavier snowfall. The best chance for accumulating snow \nremains over the San Juans but accumulations are expected to be \nmostly light and be below advisory levels. \n\nDRY AGAIN MIDWEEK, ACTIVE WEATHER POSSIBLE FOR END OF THE WEEK:\n\nBy Tuesday morning, the trough will move east of our \narea, and ridging builds in. Conditions through Wednesday are \nexpected to be dry with temperatures remaining near or slightly \nbelow normal. The rest of the forecast period may remain active\nwith some deterministic guidance showing another weak system \nfor Thanksgiving Day. Northwesterly flow will primarily bring \nimpacts to the Park Range, but exact details are uncertain at \nthis time. Another system is possible next weekend, but ensemble\nmembers are still showing significant disagreement. Stay tuned \non potential impacts to holiday travel plans.",
